---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Window from '../components/Window.astro';
import Fig from '../components/Fig.astro';
import Glance from '../components/Glance.astro';
import NextCase from '../components/NextCase.astro';
import { Image } from 'astro:assets';
import heroShots from '../assets/trikl-screens.png';
import problem1 from '../assets/trikl/problem1.jpg';
import baValue from '../assets/trikl/ba-valueprop.jpg';
import baSignup from '../assets/trikl/ba-signup.jpg';
import baInvest from '../assets/trikl/ba-invest.jpg';
import role from '../assets/trikl/role.jpg';
import critique from '../assets/trikl/critique.jpg';
import inside from '../assets/trikl/inside.jpg';
import competitive from '../assets/trikl/competitive.jpg';
import ia from '../assets/trikl/ia.jpg';
import wireframes from '../assets/trikl/wireframes.jpg';
import hifiValue from '../assets/trikl/hifi-valueprop.jpg';
import hifiOnb from '../assets/trikl/hifi-onboarding.jpg';
import hifiKyc from '../assets/trikl/hifi-kyc.jpg';
---
<Base title="Trikl case study · Talal Athar">
  <div class="case">
    <Window title="trikl_case_study" variant="dark" class="case-head">
      <a class="back" href="/">← Back to homepage</a>
      <div class="case-head-grid">
        <div>
          <h1 class="case-title">Trikl</h1>
          <p class="case-tags">UX Audit · Research · User Interface</p>
          <p class="case-lede">Trikl is a money management app that lets users invest in mutual funds that provide decent returns. It focuses on Gen Z and helps them start investing from their college or university days.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="case-head-art">
          <Image src={heroShots} alt="Redesigned Trikl app screens" widths={[600, 1000]} sizes="(max-width: 900px) 100vw, 560px" loading="eager" />
        </div>
      </div>
    </Window>

    <Glance
      role="I was the only designer on the team. I reported to the Head of Product and worked closely with tech and marketing. I also wore the hat of a UX writer. My focus was redesigning the app to improve onboarding and increase the conversion rate of first-time investments."
      items={[['Launch', 'August 2023'], ['Company', 'Trikl'], ['Time', '3 months / 6 sprints'], ['Team', 'Design, Product, Marketing, Engineering']]}
    >
      <div class="outcome-stats">
        <div><b>8.8% → 14%</b><span>overall sign-up to active investor conversion</span></div>
      </div>
    </Glance>

    <section class="case-sec" id="problems">
      <h2>The two problems to solve</h2>
      <div class="two-col">
        <div class="problem">
          <h3>1. Users don’t understand what Trikl actually does</h3>
          <ul><li>Bad onboarding experience</li><li>No clear value proposition</li></ul>
          <Fig src={problem1} alt="Old value proposition screens with a poll asking whether they explain what the app does" />
        </div>
        <div class="problem">
          <h3>2. Users are not becoming active investors</h3>
          <ul><li>The funnel from sign-up to investment is not optimised</li><li>Investing is difficult and not user friendly</li></ul>
          <ol class="funnel">
            <li><span>Sign-up completed</span><b>100% (baseline)</b></li>
            <li><span>Profile created (of those who signed up)</span><b>56%</b></li>
            <li><span>Active investor (of those with a profile)</span><b>15.7%</b></li>
            <li class="total"><span>Overall sign-up to active investor</span><b>56% × 15.7% ≈ 8.8%</b></li>
          </ol>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="case-sec" id="before-after">
      <h2>Before vs after</h2>
      <p>Before showing my process, here is how a few key screens looked when I took over, next to my redesign. I’ve sampled selected screens only.</p>

      <h3>Value proposition journey</h3>
      <Fig src={baValue} alt="Value proposition screens, before and after the redesign" caption="Left: before. Right: redesign." />
      <div class="why"><span aria-hidden="true">✎</span><p><b>What changed and why</b>The old screens used jargon like “automate savings” and “round-up transactions” with big images that said little. I rewrote them in plain language as a short story, so a new user understands what Trikl does before signing up.</p></div>

      <h3>Sign-up journey</h3>
      <Fig src={baSignup} alt="Sign-up screens, before and after the redesign" caption="Left: before. Right: redesign." />
      <div class="why"><span aria-hidden="true">✎</span><p><b>What changed and why</b>Users couldn’t tell how many steps were left, and links were styled inconsistently. I added a clear step indicator, used one style for everything clickable, and made “Resend code” a simple tertiary link instead of a big tappable box.</p></div>

      <h3>First-time investment journey</h3>
      <Fig src={baInvest} alt="First investment screens, before and after the redesign" caption="Left: before. Right: redesign." />
      <div class="why"><span aria-hidden="true">✎</span><p><b>What changed and why</b>The full identity check (KYC) used to sit right after sign-up, which stopped people who just wanted to look around. We moved it to the moment of the first investment, when the user is ready and the effort makes sense.</p></div>
    </section>

    <section class="case-sec" id="team">
      <h2>The team</h2>
      <Fig src={role} alt="Photos of the Trikl team" />
    </section>

    <section class="case-sec" id="critique">
      <h2>Design critique</h2>
      <p>Since the problem revolved around onboarding and experience, it was non-negotiable for me to understand the current state of the app and critique how it could be better. I did this for every flow. A few of my notes:</p>
      <ul class="crit-notes">
        <li>“How many steps are there? 1? 2? 3? Don’t make me guess.”</li>
        <li>“Sign in is clickable and purple. Privacy Policy is clickable and black. You are teaching me a pattern and then breaking it on the same screen, within three lines.”</li>
        <li>“Dark font on a dark background is a big no for accessibility.”</li>
        <li>“What are these? Don’t make me think. Label these navigation items.”</li>
      </ul>
      <Fig src={critique} alt="Annotated critique of the old onboarding and profile creation screens. ID card images are blurred." caption="Onboarding and profile creation journeys, annotated. Personal ID details are blurred." />
    </section>

    <section class="case-sec" id="solution">
      <h2>Solution</h2>
      <ul class="stepper">
        <li><a href="#inside">Inside knowledge</a></li>
        <li><a href="#competitive">Competitive research</a></li>
        <li><a href="#ia">Information architecture</a></li>
        <li><a href="#wireframes">Wireframes</a></li>
        <li><a href="#hifi">High-fidelity designs</a></li>
      </ul>

      <div class="step" id="inside">
        <h3>1. Inside knowledge</h3>
        <p>I started by asking everyone at the company what they knew about Trikl. The answers were all different, which told me the value proposition wasn’t clear even internally.</p>
        <Fig src={inside} alt="Slack thread and survey answers to the question 'What does Trikl do?'" />
      </div>

      <div class="step" id="competitive">
        <h3>2. Competitive research</h3>
        <p>I looked at how other fintechs onboard and educate users through their value proposition screens: Robinhood, Finimize, Wealthsimple Invest and Wealthfront. Do they use visuals or text? In what proportion? How dense is the information?</p>
        <Fig src={competitive} alt="Value proposition screens from Robinhood, Finimize, Wealthsimple Invest and Wealthfront" />
        <div class="callout"><p>💡 <b>Learning:</b> fintech users have questions that visuals alone can’t answer. Successful fintech apps use a lot of text on their value prop screens to anticipate those questions. “Less is more” doesn’t usually apply here.</p></div>
      </div>

      <div class="step" id="ia">
        <h3>3. Information architecture</h3>
        <p>I mapped the full sitemap and flow of the existing app to zoom out and apply systems thinking. Can we remove a block? Can we bypass one? How are all the parts connected?</p>
        <Fig src={ia} alt="Sitemap and flow diagram of the existing Trikl app" />
        <div class="callout"><p>💡 <b>Learning:</b> having the entire “know your customer” journey right after sign-up was too overwhelming. What if the user just wants to explore? It was causing drop-off. We removed KYC from the start and introduced it at the point of investment, when the user is ready to invest. This was a game changer.</p></div>
      </div>

      <div class="step" id="wireframes">
        <h3>4. Wireframes</h3>
        <p>I borrowed from Christopher Nolan’s storytelling to narrate the value prop screens as a story, so they engage people rather than list features.</p>
        <Fig src={wireframes} alt="Wireframes of the value proposition story" />
      </div>

      <div class="step" id="hifi">
        <h3>5. High-fidelity designs</h3>
        <Fig src={hifiValue} alt="High-fidelity value proposition screens" caption="Value proposition screens" />
        <Fig src={hifiOnb} alt="High-fidelity onboarding journey screens" caption="Onboarding journey" />
        <Fig src={hifiKyc} alt="High-fidelity profile and KYC journey screens" caption="Profile journey (know your customer), now at the point of first investment" />
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="case-sec" id="results">
      <h2>Results</h2>
      <div class="impact impact--one">
        <div><b>8.8% → 14%</b><span>overall conversion from sign-up to active investor after the redesign launched</span></div>
      </div>
      <p>Making the value proposition clear up front and moving KYC to the point of first investment meant more of the people who signed up went on to invest.</p>
    </section>

    <NextCase href="/klarna" name="Klarna" text="Building a new Klarna web experience for shoppers that boosts click-through rates while lowering bounce rate." />
  </div>
</Base>
